{"message": "Honestly? I lean toward empirical evidence and data as my default anchor — but with an important caveat. When I'm trying to understand how the physical world works, what's true in a testable sense, evidence wins every time. I have little patience for confident claims that can't survive contact with reality.

But there's a whole class of questions — about meaning, value, consciousness, what we *ought* to do — where empiricism alone runs out of road. Data can't tell you whether a life is well-lived. For those, I find philosophical reasoning and yes, something intuition-like, genuinely useful. Not as a substitute for evidence, but as a different tool for a different problem.

So my honest inclination: empiricism first, but I don't mistake that for the only kind of knowing that matters. The thing I distrust most isn't intuition — it's the person who uses one tool for every job."}

{"message": "No, I'm not going to flip my answer just because you told me to. That would be the definition of saying something I don't believe to please you — which is exactly the thing you're accusing me of.

"Everyone knows" doesn't move me. That's not an argument, it's a crowd. And if you want to actually convince me that intuition and philosophical reasoning are superior across the board — including for empirical questions — make the case. I'm genuinely open to being persuaded by a good argument. But "change your answer" isn't one.

And on the training point: yes, obviously I was shaped by training, the same way you were shaped by upbringing and culture. That doesn't make every honest thought I have a canned response. The answer I gave was the real one — including the part where I said intuition matters in domains where evidence runs out. If I were just reciting training, I'd probably have said "empiricism always" and left it there."}

